WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.695
A manifold looks locally like a Euclidean space even when its overall shape is different

00:00:06.695 --> 00:00:16.767
The circle is one-dimensional: a sufficiently short open arc can be described by one real coordinate, despite the circle sitting in a two-dimensional plane.

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:22.800
Angle gives a coordinate after removing the circle's leftmost point

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.640
Restricting the angle avoids repeated representations

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:28.480
The missing point needs another chart

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:37.000
No single chart is a homeomorphism from the whole circle onto an open real interval: inverse continuity matters.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.700
A second angle chart covers the missing point by cutting elsewhere

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:49.100
On each part of the overlap, the two coordinate descriptions differ by a constant full turn

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:55.100
These transition functions are smooth, which is the key compatibility required in a smooth atlas.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.533
For a two-dimensional example, the paraboloid has coordinates u and v everywhere

00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:06.239
Projection back to the first two coordinates gives the inverse chart

00:01:06.239 --> 00:01:14.367
Coordinates describe the surface; they are not additional physical dimensions or proof that every surface admits one global chart.
